---
/* Notes and caveats, full width below the article, as collapsed accordions.
 *
 * Everything here is static: none of it depends on what the user types, so all
 * of it is derived at build time and rendered into the HTML rather than by the
 * client script. The one exception is the all-regions table, whose rows carry
 * the cost verdict and so are built by scripts/table.ts.*/
import type { Data, Region } from "../scripts/state";

interface Props {
  data: Data;
}
const { data } = Astro.props;

const constants = data.constants;
const headline = constants.headline_indicator;
const surveyYear = data.sources.dhs.survey_year;
const regionCount = data.regions.length;

const gradientOf = (region: Region) => (region.quintiles || {})[headline] || null;

// The regions with no targeting distortion, and why each one has none. Computed,
// never counted by hand -- an earlier version of this page stated the number in
// prose and recomputed it in script, four paragraphs apart, and the two agreed
// only by luck.
const noAnswer = data.regions
  .filter((region) => (gradientOf(region)?.targeting_distortion ?? null) === null)
  .map((region) => ({
    name: region.name,
    reason: gradientOf(region)?.pending_reason || "no regional breakdown",
  }));
const withAnswer = regionCount - noAnswer.length;

// Years elapsed since the survey, as of this build.
const surveyAge = Number(data.generated.slice(0, 4)) - surveyYear;

// The household-vs-personal spread quoted in the caveats.
const spread = (key: string) => {
  const q = data.national.wealth_gradient[key].by_quintile;
  const poorest = q[0].value ?? 0;
  const richest = q[4].value ?? 0;
  return { poorest, richest, fold: richest / poorest };
};
const womanPhone = spread("phone_own_f");
const householdPhone = spread(headline);

const NUMBER_WORD = ["No", "One", "Two", "Three", "Four", "Five", "Six", "Seven",
  "Eight", "Nine", "Ten"];
const inWords = (n: number) => NUMBER_WORD[n] ?? String(n);
const pct1 = (v: number) => v.toFixed(1) + "%";

const findex = data.national.findex as {
  account_ownership: {
    indicator_name: string;
    latest_year: number;
    observations: { year: number; value: number }[];
  };
};
const accounts = findex.account_ownership;

---

<section class="notes-sect">
  <h2>Notes &amp; caveats</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    How this page can be wrong, the full table, and where every number comes from.
  </p>

  <details>
    <summary>
      {inWords(noAnswer.length)} region{noAnswer.length === 1 ? "" : "s"} have no
      distortion answer &mdash; and they are not random
    </summary>
    <p>
      <strong>{withAnswer} of {regionCount}</strong> regions have a usable targeting
      distortion. The other <strong>{noAnswer.length}</strong> do not, and are shown as
      unavailable rather than estimated. Wealth quintiles are defined <em>nationally</em>,
      so a wealthy region holds almost no bottom-quintile households and a poor one almost
      no top-quintile households. The cells that vanish are at both ends of the
      distribution &mdash; including Antananarivo capital, which the survey records with no
      bottom-quintile households at all. Those are exactly the regions where a targeted
      programme most wants an answer. Nothing is estimated in their place.
    </p>
    <ul>
      {noAnswer.map((row) => <li><strong>{row.name}</strong> &mdash; {row.reason}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p>
      Suppression follows DHS&rsquo;s own conventions, not this project&rsquo;s, and is not
      configurable: every region &times; quintile cell carries its unweighted case count,
      cells below <strong>{constants.min_cases_flag}</strong> cases are flagged &#9888; and
      shown, cells below <strong>{constants.min_cases_suppress}</strong> are suppressed and
      show &ldquo;&mdash;&rdquo;. Where any cell in a region is suppressed, the distortion
      for that region is withheld too: it is a share of a total, and a total missing one of
      its five parts would understate the denominator and flatter the result.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Distortion is computed on one survey&rsquo;s sample, not measured</summary>
    <p>
      A regional targeting distortion rests on five cells of a few hundred households each.
      Cells between {constants.min_cases_suppress} and {constants.min_cases_flag} unweighted
      cases are shown but flagged, and they are noisy: a handful of households moving
      between quintiles would shift the figure materially. Treat the ranking as informative
      and the second decimal place as not.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>The DHS figures are self-reported, and are from {surveyYear}</summary>
    <p>
      Respondents were asked whether their household owns a mobile telephone; nobody
      checked. {inWords(surveyAge)} years have passed and ownership has almost certainly
      risen. The direction of that error is knowable; the size is not. Whether the
      <em>gradient</em> has flattened over those years is not knowable at all from this
      data.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Household ownership is not personal access</summary>
    <p>
      The headline DHS indicator counts households containing a phone. Individual ownership
      among women is far lower &mdash; nationally {pct1(womanPhone.poorest)} in the poorest
      quintile against {pct1(womanPhone.richest)} in the richest, a
      {" "}{womanPhone.fold.toFixed(1)}-fold spread against
      {" "}{householdPhone.fold.toFixed(1)}-fold for households. The household figure hides
      an intra-household gap that runs the same way as everything else here.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>The cost model treats all excluded households as alike</summary>
    <p>
      The arithmetic behind the cost line assumes one in-person cost per enrollment
      regardless of who is being reached. In practice the households remote enrollment
      excludes are disproportionately the remote, dispersed and poor ones &mdash; the
      expensive ones. A single flat in-person cost therefore understates the cost of the
      residual, and understates it most in exactly the regions where the distortion is
      worst. Modelling travel time and settlement dispersion would fix this and is not yet
      built.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>These are survey regions, and the population is modelled</summary>
    <p>
      DHS {surveyYear} survey regions will not match your districts or catchment areas, and
      Madagascar&rsquo;s administrative divisions have changed since the survey. Analamanga
      appears split into the capital and everything else, which is a DHS artefact.
    </p>
    <p>
      Population figures use WorldPop&rsquo;s constrained {data.sources.worldpop.year}
      {" "}raster, which is a model. Madagascar&rsquo;s last census was 2018.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>What is not measured here at all</summary>
    <p>
      Whether people hold the identity documents a remote process would ask them to
      photograph. Whether they would trust one. Whether a household that owns a phone would
      let the eligible member use it. Network coverage. These decide
      whether remote enrollment is <em>appropriate</em>; this page addresses only whether it
      is <em>arithmetically possible</em>.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>All {regionCount} regions &mdash; the full table</summary>
    <p class="table-hint">
      Column headers sort. &ldquo;&mdash;&rdquo; means no value exists &mdash; never zero,
      never imputed. Selecting a row opens that region&rsquo;s detail.
    </p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr data-bind="table-head"></tr></thead>
        <tbody data-bind="table-body"></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>National reference &mdash; Global Findex</summary>
    <p>
      A sanity check on whether the regional picture aggregates to something plausible.
      {accounts.indicator_name}.
    </p>
    <ul class="findex-series">
      {accounts.observations.slice().reverse().map((obs) => (
        <li><span class="yr">{obs.year}</span><span class="val">{obs.value.toFixed(1)}%</span></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p>
      Findex is a periodic survey, so the years between these are genuinely absent rather
      than flat, and are not drawn. The fall between {accounts.observations[1].year} and
      {" "}{accounts.observations[0].year} is as published and has not been smoothed.
      Every source behind this page is listed in full at the foot of it.
    </p>
  </details>
</section>
